          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis described in this video, what is the earl</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the pathogenesis of atherosclerosis described in this video, what is the earliest visible lesion formed in the arterial wall?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fibrous plaque with a lipid-rich core</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fibrous plaque develops later, when smooth muscle proliferation and collagen deposition build a cap over the accumulated foam cells. It is a more advanced lesion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fatty streak composed of foam cells</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Macrophages engulf oxidised LDL without a shut-off mechanism, swelling into foam cells. Their accumulation forms the fatty streak — the earliest visible sign of atherosclerosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcified atherosclerotic plaque</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Calcification is a feature of advanced, complicated plaques and occurs much later in the disease process, not at the earliest stage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Intraluminal thrombus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thrombus formation is a complication of plaque rupture — the final catastrophic event causing myocardial infarction or stroke, not an early lesion.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the atherosclerosis sequence: LDL enters the arterial wall → gets oxidised → macrophages engulf it and become foam cells → foam cells accumulate as fatty streaks, the earliest visible lesion."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly right. Foam cells forming fatty streaks are the critical first step — this is why lowering LDL, which drives the entire sequence, sits at the centre of treatment."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
