          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with familial hypercholesterolaemia discovers she is 8 weeks</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 32-year-old woman with familial hypercholesterolaemia discovers she is 8 weeks pregnant. Her LDL-C is 210 mg/dL. Which lipid-lowering drug class is the most appropriate choice?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>High-intensity statin (atorvastatin)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Statins are contraindicated in pregnancy because cholesterol is essential for foetal development — blocking its synthesis can harm the foetus.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bile acid sequestrant (cholestyramine)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bile acid sequestrants are not absorbed systemically and are considered safe in pregnancy, making them the preferred choice when lipid-lowering is needed during gestation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>PCSK9 inhibitor (evolocumab)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PCSK9 inhibitors are monoclonal antibodies without established safety data in pregnancy. They are reserved for refractory hypercholesterolaemia in non-pregnant patients.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ezetimibe</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Ezetimibe lacks established safety in pregnancy and is not recommended. Unlike bile acid sequestrants, it is absorbed systemically.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key principle: statins are contraindicated in pregnancy because foetal development requires cholesterol. Bile acid sequestrants, which are not absorbed systemically, are the safer alternative."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Bile acid sequestrants act locally in the gut without systemic absorption, avoiding foetal exposure — a key pharmacological advantage during pregnancy."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
