          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Warfarin takes 3–5 days to reach its full anticoagulant effect after starting th</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Warfarin takes 3–5 days to reach its full anticoagulant effect after starting therapy. Which of the following best explains this delay?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Warfarin must be metabolised to its active form by hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Warfarin is already pharmacologically active when absorbed. The delay is not due to metabolic activation but to the persistence of pre-formed clotting factors in the circulation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pre-existing functional clotting factors must be cleared from the circulation before the anticoagulant effect becomes apparent</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Warfarin blocks the synthesis of NEW vitamin K–dependent clotting factors in the liver, but factors already circulating continue to function until they are naturally degraded — a process taking 3–5 days.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Warfarin needs to accumulate in adipose tissue before reaching a therapeutic plasma concentration</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Warfarin is highly protein-bound in plasma, not sequestered in adipose tissue. The delay reflects the half-lives of pre-existing clotting factors, not drug distribution.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Antithrombin III levels must rise before warfarin can exert its effect</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Antithrombin III is the cofactor for heparin's mechanism, not warfarin's. Warfarin acts independently by inhibiting vitamin K recycling in the liver.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: warfarin blocks the PRODUCTION of new clotting factors by inhibiting vitamin K recycling. The delay occurs because existing functional clotting factors must be naturally degraded before the full anticoagulant effect is seen."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. This delayed onset is why heparin — which acts immediately via antithrombin III — is used as a bridge until warfarin reaches therapeutic levels."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
